outputs.exact_statement:
After a proper surjection $r:P\to S$, with $P$ a smooth compact connected manifold, there is a dense open $P^\circ\subseteq r^{-1}(U)$ on which the marked klt family has a finite étale cover and a product decomposition $$\label{spread:family-product}
 (\mathscr J,D_{\mathscr J})
 \simeq
 (\mathscr Q,C)\times_{P^\circ}
 \mathscr T_1\times_{P^\circ}\cdots\times_{P^\circ}\mathscr T_j .$$ Here all families are proper and smooth with connected fibers. They have the following properties.*

1.  *The pair $(\mathscr Q,C)$ is a projective family of effective klt SNC pairs. It is embedded in $P^\circ\times\mathbb P^N$ and is the pullback of universal marked families by the restriction of a holomorphic map from $P$ to a fixed product of projective Hilbert schemes.*

2.  *Each $\mathscr T_i$ is a family of submanifolds of a fixed compact Kähler manifold $V$, of positive dimension $n_i$. Its cohomological local systems have their integral lattices modulo torsion and flat real polarizations. Its top Hodge line has rank one and is given by $$\lambda_i
     =(\pi_i)_*\omega_{\mathscr T_i/P^\circ}
     =F^{n_i}R^{n_i}(\pi_i)_*\mathbb C$$ Each index is of one of the following two types: $$\begin{array}{ll}
     \text{torus type:}&
     h^{1,0}(T_i)=n_i,\quad
     \bigwedge^{n_i}F^1R^1(\pi_i)_*\mathbb C
           \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\lambda_i;\\[2mm]
     \text{symplectic type:}&
     n_i\ \text{even},\quad h^{2,0}(T_i)=1,\quad
     \bigl(F^2R^2(\pi_i)_*\mathbb C\bigr)^{\otimes n_i/2}
           \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\lambda_i .
     \end{array}$$ The displayed maps are cup products on the underlying variations.*


3.  *On every fiber, $m(K_Q+C)$ and $mK_{T_i}$ are trivial. With $$\mathcal G_{m,Q}
       =(\pi_Q)_*\mathcal O_{\mathscr Q}
                      \bigl(m(K_{\mathscr Q/P^\circ}+C)\bigr),$$ there is an identification of actual holomorphic line bundles $$\label{spread:volume-line}
     r^*\mathcal G_m
       \simeq \mathcal G_{m,Q}\otimes
                       \bigotimes_i\lambda_i^{\otimes m}.$$*


4.  *If the finite cover in <a href="#spread:family-product" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="spread:family-product">[spread:family-product]</a> has degree $e$, the norm in <a href="#spread:residue-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="spread:residue-norm">[spread:residue-norm]</a> satisfies $$\label{spread:norm-product}
     \|s\|_{\mathrm{res}}
       =c\,\|s_Q\|_{Q,\mathrm{res}}\,
                       \prod_i\|\omega_i\|_{\mathrm{Hdg}}^m
     \quad\text{when}\quad
     s\longleftrightarrow
           s_Q\otimes\bigotimes_i\omega_i^{\otimes m}.$$ Here $c>0$ is constant; with compatible volume conventions, $c=e^{-m/2}$. The norm on the left gives generators and their duals of sufficiently divisible powers of $r^*(mM_S)$ the two-sided logarithmic bounds of Lemma <a href="#spread:residue-growth" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="spread:residue-growth">16</a>.*

*These assertions are preserved on the good open under further smooth compact auxiliary base changes. Neither generic finiteness of $r$ nor a global Kähler form on $P$ is required.

Use the notation of Proposition <a href="#spread:product" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="spread:product">20</a>, and write $P^\circ\subset P$ for its good open set. After a proper modification $\pi:P'\to P$, with $P'$ smooth compact, and a further shrinking of $P^\circ$, there are a positive integer $a$, a globally generated holomorphic line bundle $\mathcal H_Q$ on $P'$, and a specified isomorphism $$\label{compare:open-identification}
 \mathcal H_Q|_{\pi^{-1}(P^\circ)}
 \simeq
 \bigl(\pi^*\mathcal G_{m,Q}\bigr)^{\otimes a}
       |_{\pi^{-1}(P^\circ)} .$$ Transport to the left the $a$-th power of the volume norm $$N_{m,Q}(s)=
 \left(\int_{Q_x}|s|^{2/m}\right)^{m/2}.$$ For any holomorphic disk in $P'$ whose punctured disk lies in the good open, a local generator of the pulled-back $\mathcal H_Q$ and its dual have norm bounded by powers of $1+|\log|t||$, after a finite power substitution if necessary.

Let $t:\mathcal T\to B^\circ$ be a smooth proper family of connected compact complex manifolds of dimension $n$ over a complex manifold $B^\circ$, and suppose a closed real $(1,1)$-form on $\mathcal T$ restricts to a Kähler form on every fiber. Its restriction class $\kappa$ is flat. The cohomological variations, with their integral lattices modulo torsion, are real-polarizable. In particular:*

1.  *on $H^1$, a polarization is $$B_1(\alpha,\beta)=\int_{\mathcal T_b}
                      \alpha\wedge\beta\wedge\kappa^{n-1};$$*


2.  *if $n\geq2$, write $H^2_{\mathbb R}=P^2_{\mathbb R}\oplus\mathbb R\kappa$, where $P^2_{\mathbb R}=\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(\kappa^{n-1}\cup-)$. For $\alpha=\alpha_0+a\kappa$ and $\beta=\beta_0+b\kappa$, a full weight-two polarization is $$\label{periods:full-h2}
     B_2(\alpha,\beta)=
     \int_{\mathcal T_b}\alpha_0\wedge\beta_0\wedge\kappa^{n-2}
     -ab\int_{\mathcal T_b}\kappa^n.$$ If $h^{2,0}=1$, this real form has signature $(2,h^{1,1})$.

Let a pure real-polarizable integral variation be defined on the complement of an SNC divisor in a complex manifold, with unipotent local monodromy. Canonical Hodge extensions commute with tensor products, exterior powers, duals, and holomorphic pullbacks from smooth manifolds with SNC boundary, whose boundary complements map into the original complement. This includes disks whose punctured disks map into that complement. A morphism of pure real variations induces an isomorphism between its coimage and image on these extensions. In particular, an isomorphism of highest Hodge lines induced by such a morphism remains an isomorphism after extension.*


*If $\mathcal H$ is a highest Hodge line, $\gamma:\Delta\to B$ is a disk with $\gamma(\Delta^*)$ in the open set, and $e$ is a nowhere-zero local section of $\gamma^*\overline{\mathcal H}$, there are constants $c>0$ and $a\geq0$ such that, for $0<|z|$ sufficiently small, $$\label{periods:logbounds}
 c^{-1}(1+|\log|z||)^{-a}
 \ \leq\ \|e(z)\|\ \leq\
 c(1+|\log|z||)^a.$$ The same assertion holds for its powers and their duals.
